Topic 1. 2.5: Mitigation techniques

Domain 2 so far has been the attacker’s side: who attacks, how they get in, what’s vulnerable, and what the clues look like. 2.5 switches to defence. 
It covers how organisations reduce the chance of an attack working, and limit the damage if it does.

In Messer videos it splits 2.5 into three videos, roughly:
Segmentation and access control
Mitigation techniques (patching, encryption, monitoring, least privilege and so on)
Hardening techniques (locking down individual devices)

2.5 #1: Segmentation and access control
Segmentation
Splitting a network into separate sections instead of one big open space. If an attacker gets into one section, they can’t easily reach the rest. This links straight back 
to lateral movement, which you met with privilege escalation. Segmentation is what stops it. Segmentation can be physical (separate hardware) or logical(VLANs, from Network+
).Common examples:keeping guest Wi-Fi apart from staff systems, or medical devices on their own segment away from the general office network. Analogy: a hospital doesn’t 
let anyone wander from the café into theatre or the pharmacy. Wards have locked doors between them, so a problem in one area doesn’t spread through the whole building.

Access control
Deciding who or what is allowed to reach what. ACLs (access control lists): a list of rules saying what’s allowed or denied. On a firewall or router, that might be 
“allow traffic from this IP to this port, block everything else.” On a file or folder, it’s who can read, write or change it. Think of your Windows folder permissions 
days. Least privilege fits here too: give people only the access they need for their job and nothing more.

Application allow list / deny list
Controlling which programs are allowed to run on a computer. Allow list: only approved programs can run, and everything else is blocked. This is very secure, but it
takes work to maintain.  Deny list: everything can run except known bad programs. It’s easier to manage, but anything new or unknown slips through. (This is how traditional
antivirus works.) Analogy: an allow list is a guest list at the door, so if you’re not on it, you’re not coming in. A deny list is a list of banned people, so anyone not 
on it walks straight in, including strangers.

Isolation
Taking it one step further than segmentation: completely cutting something off. For example, moving an infected PC into a quarantine network so it can’t talk to
anything else while it’s investigated. You’ll see this again in incident response.

What is network segmentation, and how does it help stop an attacker who has already got in?
The walls need rules. Segmentation only works if traffic between segments is controlled, by a firewall or ACLs. If you setup a guest wifi on most home routers’ this 
guest Wi-Fi blocks access to your main network by default.
Client isolation. Many routers also have an option, often called AP isolation, that stops devices on the guest network from seeing each other.
Smart devices are a good fit for it too. Smart TVs, plugs and other IoT gadgets are often poorly secured, so plenty of people put those on the guest or separate network
as well. Creating a guest wifi like a “second wall” is: defence in depth. It means multiple layers of protection, so if one fails, the next one still holds. 
what’s the difference between an application allow list and a deny list, and which is more secure?
you can never list every bad thing in existence, so a deny list always has gaps, especially for brand-new malware nobody has added yet. An allow list blocks anything unknown by default.
The trade-off. An allow list is more secure but harder to manage. Every new program, and sometimes every update, has to be approved, or staff get stuck waiting. That’s why you mostly see strict allow lists on locked-down systems like kiosks, medical equipment or point-of-sale terminals, rather than on every staff laptop.

How the bouncer recognises an app:
Hash: the file’s fingerprint (back to #13). It’s very precise, but any update changes the hash, so the list needs updating too.
Certificate / publisher: “allow anything digitally signed by Microsoft.” That’s easier to maintain.
Path: “allow anything in C:\Program Files.” It’s simple, but weaker if an attacker can write to that folder.
Network zone: allow apps depending on where they’re run from (for example, the internal network vs the internet).
For Windows tools like AppLocker work well. 
